###
# Kobo Modules (a new way to see Namespaces)
Kobo organizes code into modules and namespaces to support modularity, reuse, and clear separation of concerns. 
Modules provide logical units for organizing related types, functions, and other declarations, while namespaces 
establish hierarchical identities that prevent naming conflicts across larger applications. Unlike C#, Kobo uses forward 
slashes (`/`) rather than periods (`.`) to separate namespace segments, providing a path-like naming convention. The `import` 
keyword makes external namespaces and types accessible, supporting wildcard imports such as `import * from System;` and selective 
imports of individual types. This approach simplifies dependency management, 
promotes explicit code organization, and enables seamless interoperability with existing .NET namespaces and libraries.

In kobo modules equate to namespaces in other .NET langauges.  You import all types
in a given scope as follows:
###
import * from System //normal import

###
You can group a number of modules together in one import statement as shown below.
###

//hierarchy import
import * from
{
    System/Net,
    System/Collections/Generic,
    System/Linq
}

###
Note that in kobo the member access operator does not double as an operator for traversing
namespace names.  This lends itself to one of the tenets of kobo, **consistency**.  Kobo
modules (namespaces) are structured using the forward slash (like folders, or urls). This pattern
is a more consistent mechanism for identifying which types are being brought into scope.
You can also bring specific types into scope, or bring more than one type in scope at a time.
###

//This is technically legal but basically is uneccassary.  If you're not aliasing the type
//just import the whole namespace
import Console from System 

//import more than one type at a time
import { File, Directory } from System/IO

###
and you can alias that type with a different name
###

//aliasing (usually used in scenarios where there is namespace collision)
import Console as SysConsole from System 


###
If you want to expose the static members of a type so that they can be called like global
functions you can use ```import static``` method
###


//static imports
import static Math from System


###
Declaring a module is as simple as defining it in the manner below.  **Note that modules are not allowed in scripts (the code is
meant to be run not used)***.  
> Note: Module scopes are separated by the forwad slash (/) not the member access operator (.) as in c#
###

//applies scope to code (like namespace)
module Example/Namespaces 

###
Because member acces operator is not used for giving the fully qualified type name you cannot give the explicit name of a type inline 
while using the type in kobo.  So something like ```System.Console.WriteLine``` would fail.  To declare that the fully qualified name is to be
used for a given type use the ```using``` keyword as shown below:
###

//this can be placed at any level to create a fully qualified scope
//using explicitly replaces type references instead of emitting C# directives
using Math from System
using Math as M from System //aliasing it

###
Thes statements can be placed at any level in the program as long as it is before use.  They simply declare where a type is coming from,
which helps in scenarios where there is type ambiguity.  

The code here demonstrates the use of the module scopes created above
###


[static]
function:void Main(string[] args)
{

    var twelve = Sqrt(144)
    var twelve_2 = M.Sqrt(144)
    var twelve_3 = Math.Sqrt(256)

    var directories = Directory.GetDirectories(".")

    SysConsole.WriteLine("This is a test");
    
}